1. PROJECT OVERVIEW

Develop a full-stack AI-powered educational platform named PrepPilot – Explainable and Adaptive AI-Powered Learning Platform .

PrepPilot is designed to help students understand academic concepts, generate study notes, practise quizzes, identify mistakes, and improve their learning performance using publicly available and appropriately licensed educational resources.

The platform must integrate Generative AI, Large Language Models (LLMs), Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), semantic search, and adaptive learning techniques to provide a personalized and explainable learning experience.

Unlike ordinary AI chatbot applications that simply generate answers, PrepPilot must implement a continuous learning cycle:

LEARN → TEST → DETECT → ADAPT

LEARN: Students learn concepts through AI-generated notes and explanations.
TEST: Students practise quizzes and coding questions.
DETECT: The platform identifies incorrect answers, mistakes, and weak concepts.
ADAPT: The platform recommends personalized study materials, revision activities, and practice questions.

The main objective is to develop a reliable, explainable, personalized AI learning platform rather than a basic chatbot that calls an AI API.

PROJECT REQUIREMENTS

Follow these requirements throughout development:

1. Use only publicly available educational content with verified reuse permissions.
2. Maintain source attribution and licensing information for all educational materials.
3. Use React with JavaScript only for the frontend.
4. Use normal CSS for all styling.
5. Use Python for backend development.
6. Use FastAPI for REST API development.
7. Use SQLite for relational data storage.
8. Use sqlite-vec for vector similarity search, subject to compatibility testing.
9. Integrate Gemini and Groq APIs.
10. Use Sentence Transformers for generating text embeddings.
11. Implement RAG for source-grounded AI responses.
12. Keep frontend, backend, AI services, database logic, and reusable components separate.
13. Never hardcode API keys or credentials.
14. Use environment variables for API credentials.
15. Do not invent evaluation results, accuracy percentages, or performance claims.
16. Use mock data only during initial UI development. Replace it with real backend API responses during integration.
17. Build and test features incrementally rather than generating the entire application in one step.

